  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThe first part of the Long Term (Wednesday-Friday) is \ncharacterized by a continued heavy rain and flash flood risk. \nIsolated strong to severe storms with damaging winds remain \npossible, but risk of severe convective weather stays secondary to\nhydrological impacts. Moisture streaming from southwest to \nnortheast from the remnants of TS Rachel and northeast moving \nHurricane Simon landfalling over the Pacific Coast of MX will \nenhance rain chances from the Big Bend through the northern \nPermian Basin, with a sharp cutoff in higher rainfall from \nCulberson County into the Eddy County Plains. Similar to Tuesday, \nwhile not every place will see heavy rain, potential exists for \nswathes of at least 0.25\" to 0.50\" of rainfall. Ensembles are \nindicating 1.5\" to 2.0\" in heaviest storms both Wednesday and \nThursday, but with most likely higher amounts remaining in the \n1.0\" to 1.5\" range each day. As on previous days, coverage is \nuncertain and not every place is guaranteed to see rain, but flash\nflooding will become more of a concern in regions that see \nrepeated rounds of heavy storms where soils become more saturated.\nAt this time, central and eastern portions of the area are \noutlooked in a MRGL to SLGT risk of excessive rain from Tuesday \nuntil Friday, so we anticipate that flood watch products may need \nto be issued in subsequent shifts. With widespread cloud cover, \nhighs trend down below 90F for the lowlands and into the upper 70s\nto lower 80s F for the Guadalupes and Davis Mountains Wednesday, \nwhile lows remain in the 60s and 70s F. Then, as a cold front \nbegins to develop into the northern part of the area by Thursday, \nhighs shift further down into the mid 70s to mid 80s F as \nsoutheasterly winds shift to the northerly by the late afternoon \nthrough evening. This will be followed by lows falling below 60F, \nbelow 50F for the Guadalupes/Delawares and Davis Mountains and \nbelow 70F for the Rio Grande. \n\nThe second part of the Long Term (Friday-Saturday) will be \ncharacterized by strong (at least 35 mph) northeast winds over the\nGuadalupes/Delawares and passes and below average temperatures. \nPoPs remain high (45% to 60%) for much of the area, before \ndecreasing below 30% from northeast to southwest through Friday \nafternoon. There has been a continued signal for strong northeast \nwinds with gusts up to 50 mph for late Thursday evening-Friday \nmorning in association with a reinforcing backdoor cold front that\nwill deliver a continental polar air mass over the area. As of \nnow, ensembles depict a 35% to 50% chance of wind gusts of at \nleast 58 mph for the Guadalupes/Delawares and surrounding passes \nfor early Friday morning through early Friday afternoon, while NWP\nguidance also shows GDP meeting High Wind criteria during this \ntime period. Additionally, there is a high (greater than 70%) \nchance of winds gusting above 45 mph for much of the western \nhigher terrain and at least meeting Wind Advisory criteria, with \n25 to 35 mph in the lowlands. Ensemble spreads in temperatures \nshow a consistent signal of highs failing to rise above 70F for \nmuch of the area, settling within a most likely range of 55F-65F. \nAfter the prolonged very warm temperatures, anyone traveling \noutdoors may feel unaccustomed to this abrupt change in \ntemperature coupled with strong winds. Therefore, as rain chances \ntaper off from northeast to southwest, we are expecting Friday to \nfeel windy and cold, with wind chills down to the 30s and 40s F \nfor highest elevations of the Guadalupes/Delawares and Davis \nMountains. Lows fall largely into the 40s to lower 50s F Friday \nnight before once again largely failing to rise above 70F for \nSaturday. Thereafter, mid to upper ridging builds from the Desert \nSW and a renewed warming trend is expected into early next week.",
